Write "@" file mentions into the message, as in Claude Code.

Upstream's "@" menu turns a pick into a chip under the composer and sends it
as an "[Attached: <path>]" line before the message, so "add a test to
@src/app.ts" can't be written: the path leaves the sentence. A pick from the
menu now replaces the "@" token with "@src/app.ts" (a folder gets a trailing
"/", a path with a space is quoted) and a space, and the message is sent as
typed. Claude Code reads a mentioned file itself; Codex and the other agents
run in the workspace and open the path. The rest of a token the caret sat
inside is replaced too, so "@readm|e" becomes "@readme.md". In the composer
a path mention is coloured, drawn by a copy of the text behind the
transparent textarea, as upstream colours a slash command; that copy now
takes the textarea's exact box and scroll, since a padded theme moved
upstream's off the caret. The user bubble shows a path mention as inline
code, which opens the file when clicked and keeps "__init__.py" from turning
bold; "@alice" and code stay as typed. The menu says "insert" instead of
"attach". The file viewer's "Attach to agent" still adds a chip for a line
range, sent as before. The new-session screen's "@" menu writes into the
text the same way, and drops its chips.
Base: omnigent v0.18.0 (a8676d23e88d), after 0001-0029.

